# PathInfo is declared twice, and the two disagree

**DONE.** `PathInfo` binds `nix::ValidPathInfo`, in `decl/pathinfo.py`
beside the header that type comes from. `decl/store.py` imports it and
`query_path_info` is one line. There is one declaration of PathInfo,
and it is the real type.

## Problem

One C++ type, `nix::ValidPathInfo`, had two declarations with
different surfaces. Only one was built.

    decl/store.py     PathInfo         SHIPPED (in NANOBIND)
    decl/pathinfo.py  ValidPathInfo    read only by gates/nbcheck.py

| method | store.py | pathinfo.py |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `store_path()` | absent | `StorePath` |
| `path()` | `StorePath` | `str`, store dir joined on |
| `deriver()` | `StorePath \| None` | `str \| None` |

Three methods differed in TYPE and one existed in only one of them.
That is the F1 and F7a shape - one fact stated twice, and something
reading the wrong statement - at the scale of a whole type.

Found by Carl asking why `@cxx_parts` exists at all.

## What was done, in two steps

**First half.** `decl/pathinfo.py` was DELETED. It described a
`path()` this repo does not ship, and the corpus gate was judging a
surface nobody got. PathInfo stayed synthetic, in `decl/store.py`,
beside its producer - a synthetic value binds no Nix class, so "one
Nix class, one file" did not yet cover it.

**Second half, this file's subject.** The file was EARNED back when
PathInfo became `nix::ValidPathInfo`, because `path-info.hh` is that
type's header. Binding the real type, the move, `store_dir` joining
the wire and the hand-written `_from_parts` are one decision:
`_from_parts` cannot be written without `store_dir` on the wire, so
the wire moves with the type whatever the order. Same-version-only
(055) made that free.

## Why there were two

`decl/store.py` used `@cxx_parts`: a prelude plus one C++ expression
per declared field, which declared a POD struct `cythonix::PathInfo`
whose members ARE the wire types. That field map was a second
description of PathInfo, in a second vocabulary, on the method that
RETURNS one.

`@cxx_parts` was removed before this task ran, and the map became
designated initialisers in an ordinary body. That closed the second
vocabulary and left the second TYPE, which is what this closed.

## What binding the real type cost, and what it bought

`nix::ValidPathInfo` is not an aggregate: it has a virtual base, no
default constructor, and its only constructor takes an
`UnkeyedValidPathInfo`, which needs the store directory. Three parts
cross rendered and are parsed back. So `_from_parts` is eleven lines
of C++ where the struct's was one.

Against that: the nine-field copy on every `query_path_info` is gone,
`query_path_info` is `return *s.queryPathInfo(path);`, and there is
one PathInfo rather than two.

Five of ten accessors are DERIVED - `@reads` names the member and the
emitter writes the whole binding. The five with bodies are all the
same kind of thing: a rendering.

## Surface changes, listed rather than silent

- `store_dir` appears. New accessor, new wire field. It is the
  LOGICAL store directory - `/nix/store` even for a chroot store -
  and `real_path` still answers where the bytes are.
- `registration_time` is `int | None` where it was `int` with 0
  meaning unknown. Upstream keeps a `time_t` and spells "unknown" as
  0, which is also a real Unix time.
- The wire ORDER gains `store_dir` in second place. Everything else
  keeps its position.
- `PathInfo` moves to `cythonix_bindings.pathinfo`. The package
  re-exports it, so `from cythonix_bindings import PathInfo` is
  unchanged.

`path()` did NOT change, and that was a condition. Upstream's
`ValidPathInfo::path` is a StorePath member called `path`; an earlier
sketch renamed it to `store_path()` and gave `path()` a rendered
string, copied from the declaration this repo does not ship - which
is the mistake having two declarations causes, made while proposing
to fix it.

## What the perturbation found: the gate was half asleep

The task said to write the bijection with one field dropped and watch
the round-trip gate fail naming it. Two gates were run.

**The reader gate fired.** Deleting `u.ultimate = ultimate;` outright
stopped the BUILD:

    PathInfo._from_parts never mentions 'ultimate', which crosses the
    wire. A body that drops a part compiles and loses it in silence.

**The round-trip gate did NOT.** Keeping the mention and writing
`u.ultimate = false;` instead passed the whole suite. The gate built
its only PathInfo from an ADDED path, whose `ultimate` is already
false - so the dropped field was invisible.

The same shadow covered `deriver`, `registration_time`, `references`
and `sigs`. Five of ten parts were asleep: any part sitting at its
C++ default proves nothing, however many times it round-trips.

**The fix is a second case per type, and a rule.** Each wire value now
lists a case whose every part differs from the producer's, and the
test refuses to pass while any part holds one value across all of
them - naming the part. Both halves were perturbation-verified: the
round trip now fails on the dropped `ultimate`, and making the second
case agree with the first fails with

    PathInfo: ['ultimate'] holds one value in every case, so this test
    would pass with it dropped from _from_parts.

## What the emitter gained on the way

- `@reads` works on a plain accessor, not only on a `@property`. A
  member read cannot be bound by pointer, because `.def` takes a
  function and `&T::narSize` is not one.
- A wire value may carry its own `_from_parts` BODY. The emitter still
  owns the signature - one typed parameter per wire field, in order -
  so a written body can only consume what `_parts` sent.
- A wire value that declares no parts crosses as every accessor it
  has, in declaration order, whether or not the emitter wrote the
  struct. Listing ten names that are already ten `def`s one screen
  above is a second declaration of one fact. When an accessor must be
  kept OFF the wire, the place to say so is that accessor.
- A quoted annotation means what the same annotation means unquoted.
  `"I64 | None"` asked the emitter for a class called I64, because a
  string annotation never resolved the vocabulary.
- `produced` is its own question. `is_value` stood in for it, and
  `is_value` means "the emitter wrote the struct" - which stops being
  the same thing the moment a produced value binds a real type.

## Decided along the way

**One class one file, and the extension grain stays per header.**
`decl/pathinfo.py` compiles to `cythonix_bindings.pathinfo`, exactly
as `decl/path.py` compiles to `path`. nanobind keeps one type registry
per process and a module imports what it names, which `decl/store.py`
already relies on for StorePath - so a third extension cost nothing
and needed no new machinery. `generate.py`'s list did NOT become a
mapping of module to files; there is nothing yet that wants one.

## Not decided

Whether the wire should carry rendered or structured values in
general. `sigs`, `ca` and `nar_hash` are all rendered today, and each
is parsed back by hand. `nix::Hash` as its own message is the
alternative, and it makes the wire describe Nix's types rather than a
caller's.

`queryPathInfo` returns a cached `ref<const ValidPathInfo>` and the
binding dereferences into a copy. Holding the share is available and
is not wanted: a VALUE is what the store said when it was asked.
